The '''Burbur Era''' (1800–1832) was a period of political crisis, military conflict, and institutional collapse in the history of the [[Kingdom of Burawa]]. Named from the Burawi word for "collapse" or "breakdown," the era encompassed the turbulent reign of [[Iacob VI]], the last Burawi monarch, and concluded with his abdication and the establishment of the [[First Republic of Burawa]]. The period was marked by two major conflicts, the [[Burawi War]] (1811–1822) against the [[Burawi Kingdom of Galwa|Galwa]] and the [[Maianka War]] (1828–1831) against [[Orma]], as well as chronic political instability that ultimately destroyed the monarchy.

The Burbur Era began with the death of [[Nuur V]] on 16 March 1800 and ended with Iacob VI's abdication in 1832, after which a constitutional convention established a republican government. The contrast with the preceding [[Constitutional Era of Burawa]], remembered as a period of stability and progress, was stark.

== Accession of Iacob VI ==

[[Iacob VI]] acceded to the throne upon the death of his father [[Nuur V]] on 16 March 1800. The new king inherited a kingdom that had enjoyed nearly six decades of constitutional stability, scientific progress, and economic growth. Iacob VI, however, proved to be a ruler of markedly different temperament from his father. Where Nuur V had accepted the constraints of constitutional monarchy and worked within the parliamentary system, Iacob VI chafed at limitations on royal authority and sought to expand the crown's power.

The new king's autocratic instincts brought him into immediate conflict with parliament. The Hoga Senate and House of Commons, which had grown accustomed to exercising real power during Nuur V's long reign, resisted the king's efforts to centralise authority. The resulting political struggle defined the domestic politics of the era and gradually eroded the constitutional order established in 1743.

== Hoga factionalism ==

The Hoga aristocracy, which had dominated Burawi politics since the medieval period, was deeply divided during the Burbur Era. The divisions within the Hoga class contributed significantly to the parliamentary dysfunction that characterised Iacob VI's reign and created the conditions for both the Hoga Plot and the Rose Revolution.

The traditional Hoga families, whose wealth derived from agricultural landholdings and whose status was based on hereditary privilege, found themselves increasingly marginalised by the rise of merchant power. The commercial class, which had accumulated wealth through colonial trade and early industrial enterprise, exercised influence in the House of Commons that rivalled or exceeded that of the Hoga in the Senate. Some Hoga families adapted by investing in commercial ventures, marrying into merchant houses, or embracing the economic modernisation of the Constitutional Era. Others resisted, clinging to traditional sources of wealth and status and viewing merchant influence as a threat to the social order.

This fundamental divide produced competing factions within the Hoga class. The reformist faction, concentrated in urban and commercially connected regions, accepted the constitutional order and sought to modernise Hoga privilege rather than defend it absolutely. These families were willing to work with the merchant class and even with the king, provided that their core interests were protected. The traditionalist faction, dominant in rural highland regions and among families whose wealth was purely agricultural, viewed the constitutional order as an erosion of proper hierarchy. They were receptive to the king's autocratic ambitions, believing that a strong monarchy would restore their traditional privileges.

The factional division played out in the Hoga Senate, where reformist and traditionalist lords clashed over legislation, appointments, and policy. The Senate, which had been designed to represent a unified aristocratic interest, became instead an arena of aristocratic conflict. Deadlock in the Senate compounded deadlock between the Senate and the House of Commons, producing a legislative paralysis that the king exploited to expand executive power.

The king himself contributed to the factionalism. Iacob VI cultivated the traditionalist faction, offering promises of restored privilege in exchange for support against parliament. At the same time, he played reformist and traditionalist against each other, preventing either from consolidating enough power to effectively constrain him. The strategy served the king's short-term interests but deepened the divisions within the Hoga class and ultimately made it impossible to govern.

== Burawi War (1811–1822) ==

{{main|Burawi War}}

The [[Burawi War]] was an eleven-year conflict between the [[Kingdom of Burawa]] and the [[Burawi Kingdom of Galwa]] fought along their shared border. The war represented the most significant military confrontation between the two Burawi states since the split of 1709 and ended with the creation of the artificial buffer state of [[Kijura]].

The war originated in a series of border disputes that had festered since the Imperial Split. The precise boundary between the two kingdoms, particularly in the region where the [[Dimar River]] and [[Great Kaftian Lake]] met the northern territories, had never been definitively settled. Both sides claimed villages, pasture lands, and trade routes along the frontier. Sporadic clashes between border garrisons and local militias escalated through the first decade of the nineteenth century until, in 1811, open warfare erupted.

The conflict proved unexpectedly prolonged and costly. Neither kingdom possessed the military capacity to deliver a decisive blow. The terrain along the border, a mix of river valleys, lakeshore, and highland passes, favoured defence over offence. Campaigns were conducted in a desultory fashion, with advances and retreats measured in miles rather than provinces. The war settled into a pattern of attrition that drained the resources and patience of both kingdoms.

By 1822, both Burawa and Galwa were exhausted. Peace negotiations, mediated by the [[Cardinal of Burawa]] operating from neutral [[Weli Markos]], produced a settlement that satisfied neither side fully but ended the fighting. The border region would be separated from both kingdoms and constituted as an independent state: [[Kijura]], named for the Burawi word meaning "the middle place."

[[Lord Cusmaan]], a Galwa noble of distinguished lineage but limited political prospects in Galwa itself, was installed as the first ruler of Kijura. The arrangement removed a potential troublemaker from Galwa while providing Burawa with a buffer against its western rival. The creation of Kijura represented a novel solution to an intractable conflict, but it left both sides with unresolved grievances that would resurface in later decades.

== Maianka War (1828–1831) ==

{{main|Maianka War}}

The [[Maianka War]], also known as the Fourth Orma-Burawa War, was a three-year conflict between Burawa and the [[Kingdom of Orma]] over the status of the Maianka territory in southeastern Burawa. The war demonstrated both the continued volatility of Burawa's eastern frontier and the kingdom's capacity to project military power when sufficiently motivated.

The immediate cause of the war was a dynastic complication involving [[House Barkadole]], a prominent Hoga family of the Maianka region. The Barkadole had intermarried with Orma nobility over several generations, maintaining connections on both sides of the border. In the late 1820s, the head of House Barkadole, citing these Orma connections and growing dissatisfaction with Iacob VI's autocratic rule, formally requested permission to remove Maianka from the Burawi kingdom and align the territory with Orma.

The request was unacceptable to the Burawi crown. Maianka, which contained a portion of the [[Lake Haro]] shoreline and controlled approaches to the southeastern highlands, was too valuable to be surrendered. Iacob VI refused the request and, when the Barkadole began making preparations for departure regardless, dispatched troops to Maianka to secure the territory and arrest the Hoga leadership.

Orma, honouring its alliance with the Barkadole, intervened. Orma forces crossed the border to support the Maiankan Hoga, and fighting escalated into a general war. The conflict saw Burawa commit significant resources, motivated by both strategic necessity and the king's desire for a military victory that might bolster his domestic political position.

The war concluded in 1831 with a Burawa victory. Burawi forces pushed the Orma army back across the border and secured control of Maianka. House Barkadole was exiled, its lands confiscated and distributed to loyal Hoga families. The victory, however, proved pyrrhic. The financial cost of the war, combined with the casualties suffered and the political disruption of exiling a major Hoga house, further destabilised an already fragile political system.

== Hoga Plot ==

{{main|Hoga Plot}}

The [[Hoga Plot]] was a conspiracy between [[Iacob VI]] and a coalition of Hoga aristocrats to restore royal absolutism by dissolving parliament and reestablishing direct monarchical rule. The plot was planned before the [[Maianka War]] but was leaked during the conflict, causing internal panic within the royalist camp and contributing directly to the conditions that produced the [[Rose Revolution]].

The plot originated in the king's frustration with parliamentary opposition to his autocratic ambitions. Iacob VI, who had never accepted the constraints of constitutional monarchy, cultivated allies among Hoga lords who shared his desire to restore the old order. These aristocrats, many of whom had seen their privileges eroded by the rise of merchant power and the reforms of the Constitutional Era, believed that a restoration of royal absolutism would also restore their traditional authority.

[[House Barkadole]], the Maiankan Hoga family, had been privy to the plot in its early stages. The Barkadole leadership, however, grew increasingly alarmed by the conspiracy's implications. They feared that a coup against parliament would trigger civil war, destabilising the kingdom and potentially drawing in foreign powers. The Barkadole initially sought permission to remove Maianka from Burawi control and align with [[Orma]], in part to distance themselves from the coming crisis.

The king refused the Barkadole request. Rather than allowing Maianka to leave peacefully, Iacob VI viewed the territory's attempted departure as both a strategic loss and a betrayal by a family that had been party to the plot. The subsequent invasion of Maianka and the [[Maianka War]] was driven in part by the king's desire to prevent the Barkadole from escaping the consequences of the conspiracy they had helped plan.

During the war, details of the Hoga Plot were leaked. The exposure created panic within the royalist coalition. Military officers who had been kept ignorant of the plot felt betrayed; they had been fighting in Maianka to suppress a rebellion, not knowing that the rebellion had been precipitated by a conspiracy to overthrow the constitutional order. The revelation helped sway key military leaders away from the Hoga and toward the Liberal opposition.

The exposure of the Hoga Plot demonstrated that the king could not be trusted to abide by constitutional constraints. For [[Siciid Dhegey]] and the Liberal faction, the plot provided evidence that reform within the existing system was impossible: a monarch who conspired to overthrow parliament could not be trusted to share power. The plot thus became a catalyst for the Rose Revolution, convincing previously moderate voices that the monarchy itself had to be abolished.

The exposure of the Hoga Plot shattered the fragile equilibrium within the Hoga class. The plot had been primarily a traditionalist project; reformist Hoga families had largely been excluded. The revelation that traditionalist lords had conspired with the king to overthrow parliament drove a final wedge between the factions. Reformist Hoga lords, fearing that they too would be implicated or that the plot's failure would lead to reprisals, began to distance themselves from both the king and the traditionalist faction. Some quietly made their peace with the Liberal opposition, recognising that the Rose Revolution offered better prospects for their survival than continued alliance with a discredited monarchy.

== Political crisis and abdication ==

{{main|Rose Revolution}}

The combination of two major wars, the financial strain they produced, and Iacob VI's persistent efforts to expand royal authority had, by 1831, created a political crisis of the first order. The parliamentary system established by the Constitution of 1743 proved unable to manage the stresses of the Burbur Era. The king and parliament were locked in a struggle for supremacy that neither could definitively win.

[[Siciid Dhegey]], the leader of the Liberal faction in the House of Commons, emerged as the central figure of the opposition to Iacob VI's autocratic rule. Dhegey, a merchant's son who had risen through the Liberal ranks on the strength of his oratory and organisational skill, recognised that the existing constitutional framework could not restrain a determined monarch. The Hoga Senate, dominated by aristocrats whose interests were often aligned with the crown, served as a brake on reform rather than a check on royal power.

In early 1832, Dhegey organised a peaceful revolution to dissolve both the Hoga Senate and the monarchy. The movement drew support from the merchant class, urban professionals, reform-minded military officers, and elements of the rural population exhausted by war taxation. Demonstrations were organised in Samara and other major cities; petitions were presented to the king demanding constitutional reform; and Liberal representatives in the House of Commons refused to cooperate with royal ministers.

The revolution culminated in the king's rose garden at the royal palace. On a spring day in 1832, Iacob VI met with Dhegey and a delegation of parliamentary and military leaders in the garden. The king was presented with a choice: abdicate and allow a peaceful transition to a parliamentary republic, or face civil war. The garden, famous for its roses, gave its name to the event: the [[Rose Revolution]].

Iacob VI, lacking sufficient support to resist, chose abdication. The monarchy that had ruled Burawa since 403 AD was at an end. The Rose Revolution had achieved its objectives without bloodshed, establishing a precedent for peaceful political transition that would shape Burawi political culture for generations.

A constitutional convention was convened to draft a new framework for government. The convention, dominated by Liberal delegates but including Conservative and military representatives, produced a constitution establishing a parliamentary republic. Sovereignty was vested in the people rather than the crown. The Hoga Senate was dissolved as a hereditary body and replaced by an elected Senate, with members chosen by the propertied classes. The House of Commons retained its role as the lower house, with the franchise extended to all men who paid the poll tax. Executive power was vested in a Prime Minister responsible to parliament, rather than in a monarch. The [[First Republic of Burawa]] was proclaimed in 1832, inaugurating the [[Early Republic Era]].

== Legacy ==

The Burbur Era was remembered as a period of failure and collapse. The contrast with the preceding Constitutional Era, which had been characterised by stability, progress, and expanding prosperity, made the turbulence of Iacob VI's reign all the more striking. The monarchy, which had provided continuity and legitimacy for more than fourteen centuries, fell in a parliamentary coup with remarkably little resistance, suggesting that the institution had been hollowed out by years of conflict and mismanagement.

The creation of Kijura as a buffer state resolved one conflict but left unresolved the deeper antagonism between Burawa and Galwa. The Maianka War demonstrated that Burawa remained capable of military effectiveness, but the political fallout from the exile of House Barkadole contributed to the coalition that overthrew the king. The republican government that emerged from the constitutional convention would face the challenge of rebuilding a political order that had been shattered by three decades of crisis.

